{"id": "cabc2d79-3d08-48ef-9a15-4280fe60730b", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T06:26:58.226889", "source": "user", "parent_id": "44c6e07e-22e8-49d3-9dc4-37ef58addd28", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only deliverable (per track.md). Please complete the following initial step and return your findings; do NOT produce the final design yet. Report ONLY the items requested in this turn: (1) Input inspection, (2) Proposed approach sketch, (3) Assumptions & uncertainties. Specifically: a) Inspect /workspace and any provided documents; list relevant files, prior context, and confirm whether QuaRot codebase, calibration datasets, and model weights are locally available or must be treated as 'not available' for this design. Note that runtime has network disabled, no GPU, and limited CPU, so any design relying on actual training/quantization runs must explicitly mark those steps as feasible-only-with-external-resources. b) Propose a concise approach structure for the experiment design: study question, factors (independent variables), primary outcomes (dependent variables), candidate model family(ies), candidate evaluation tasks/metrics, candidate baseline methods, candidate quantization granularities, candidate data sources for calibration, and a high-level analysis plan (e.g., statistical comparison of perplexity/accuracy vs. bit-width, ablation isolating the Hadamard transform's contribution). Reconcile the approach with the fixed requirements: weight-only quantization; W bits in {4, 3, 2}; RTN and GPTQ as baseline methods. QuaRot's Hadamard transformation should be the treatment whose incremental value is being characterized. c) Enumerate assumptions and uncertainties: expected empirical outcome (stated as a hypothesis, not a fact), unstated resources/runtime budget, choice of evaluation suite, choice of model family, calibration data size and domain, whether KV-cache/activation quantization is in or out of scope (initial reading suggests weight-only, so confirm), how outliers/abnormal weights are handled, how 'quality' is operationalized (perplexity vs. downstream task accuracy vs. both), and statistical power considerations for low-bit comparisons. After I review this, I will either approve the approach or request focused refinements.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How does the inclusion of QuaRot's Hadamard transformation improve the quality of weight-only quantized models under low-precision settings? Can QuaRot stabilize and improve performance at 2–4 bit weight precision where traditional quantization methods like RTN and GPTQ often fail?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [\"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_5\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_7\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_11\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_16\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_17\"], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
