TQ fail?\\\") into a testable hypothesis: the \\u0394(Hadamard on \\u2212 off) should be largest at the lowest bit-width and shrink monotonically as bit-width grows.\\n\\n**Candidate factors (independent variables).**\\n1. **Quantization method** \\u2014 RTN, GPTQ (fixed by task).\\n2. **Weight bit-width** \\u2014 {4, 3, 2} (fixed by task; FP16 reference anchor).\\n3. **Hadamard treatment** \\u2014 OFF (direct quant on W) vs ON (QuaRot's random Hadamard H inserted as W \\u2192 W\\u00b7H, activations pre-multiplied by H^T at inference, with bound-and-rotate / outlier-mitigation as in the QuaRot paper).\\n4. **(Secondary) Calibration data size** \\u2014 e.g., {128, 512, 2048} C4 sequences; included to test robustness of GPTQ's dependence on calibration, where we expect Hadamard to *reduce* the dependence.\\n5. **(Secondary) Group size** \\u2014 per-channel vs per-group (group_size \\u2208 {-1, 128}); included to confirm Halen's gain is not just a workaround for a coarser granularity setting.\\n6. (Out of scope for the primary DV): KV-cache / activation quantization, weight\\u2013activation equalization variants beyond H \\u2014 flag as extension only.\\n\\n**Primary outcomes (DVs).**\\n- **Language-modeling quality:** perplexity on WikiText-2 (raw, standard split) and on a held-out C4 validation slice. (FER)\\n- **Downstream zero-shot quality:** 4\\u20135 task accuracies drawn from LM Evaluation Harness: **Hellaswag, ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, PIQA, WinoGrande**; plus optional BoolQ. Aggregate via **normalized average accuracy** as in the original QuaRot paper, so the unit of comparison matches prior literature. (FER)\\n- **Mechanistic proxy (pre-experiment justification, not a DV itself):** the L\\u221e / L2 outlier ratio of W before vs after H \\u2014 used to *predict* where Hadamard should help most.\\n\\n**Candidate model family.**\\n- Primary: **LLaMA-2-7B** (matches the family QuaRot was originally evaluated on and is the standard reference point in the literature for W2\\u2013W4 PTQ).\\n- Secondary/replication: **LLaMA-2-13B** if compute permits; cheaper smoke check: **LLaMA-3.2-1B** or **TinyLlama-1.1B** to validate pipeline.\\n- Decision needed from researcher: model family and size \\u2014 affects both fidelity of the comparison and runtime cost.\\n\\n**Candidate baselines.**\\n- FP16 (unquantized) \\u2014 upper-bound anchor. (FER)\\n- RTN, **no Hadamard** \\u2014 sequential round-to-nearest, per-channel symmetric.\\n- GPTQ, **no Hadamard** \\u2014 second-order Hessian-based, with OBQ/GroupGPTQ ordering.\\n- RTN + Hadamard (i.e., QuaRot-style rotation layered on RTN).\\n- GPTQ + Hadamard (the canonical QuaRot-on-GPTQ configuration in the paper).\\n- (Optional tertiary reference) AWQ \\u2014 *not* required, but useful as a literature sanity check; flagged because AWQ is the most-cited competing W4/W3 method and lets us place QuaRot's incremental gain in context. AWQ is **optional and not a fixed requirement** \\u2014 defer to researcher.\\n\\n**Quantization granularities.**\\n- Default: **per-channel** symmetric uniform (QuaRot paper's choice for head-to-head against GPTQ).\\n- Ablation axis: **per-group** with `group_size \\u2208 {128, -1}` to confirm effects are not granularity artifacts.\\n\\n**Calibration data.**\\n- Default: **C4** (`allenai/c4`, `en` validation) shuffled subsamples, mirroring QuaRot's setup. Sensitivity sweep over `{128, 512, 2048}` calibration sequences.\\n- For outlier analysis (pre-study, FER): can use a smaller calibration slice since no quantization decisions depend on it.\\n\\n**High-level analysis plan.**\\n1. **Pilot sanity check** (smallest model, smallest calibration slice) \\u2014 confirm the pipeline reproduces known directional results (e.g., RTN \\u226b GPTQ at W4, GPTQ > RTN by larger margin as bits drop). (FER)\\n2. **Primary 3-way factorial**: for each (model \\u00d7 method \\u00d7 bits \\u00d7 Hadamard-on/off), compute PPL and downstream accuracy. Tabulate \\u2206PPL = PPL(with-Hadamard) \\u2212 PPL(without-Hadamard) and analogously for accuracy. Plot **\\u0394 vs. bit-width** \\u2014 this is the central figure.\\n3. **Method-specific contrast**: separate plots for RTN-on vs RTN-off, and GPTQ-on vs GPTQ-off. This isolates the *incremental* contribution of Hadamard within each method, which is the literal question being asked.\\n4. **Stability at extreme bits**: focus analysis on W2 (and W3 GPTQ) where RTN/GPTQ reportedly \\\"fail.\\\" Compare whether QuaRot reduces variance across calibration seeds (for GPTQ) and across model layers.\\n5. **Calibration sensitivity**: GPTQ-on vs GPTQ-off curves at n_cal \\u2208 {128, 512, 2048}. *Hypothesis check:* Hadamard reduces slope w.r.t. n_cal (i.e., QuaRot is more robust when calibration is scarce).\\n6. **Mechanism evidence**: compute the pre/post-Hadamard outlier ratio (e.g., ||W||_\\u221e / ||W||_2 and the fraction of entries > 6\\u03c3) for each weight matrix and correlate with the observed \\u0394PPL. This is *explanatory*, not confirmatory.\\n7. **Statistics**: at each (method, bits) cell, report bootstrap 95% CIs over random calibration seeds and eval permutations. Pair the on/off cells with a dependent-samples test (paired bootstrap on \\u0394) \\u2014 appropriate because the only difference is the rotation. For the per-task accuracy endpoint, apply a paired permutation test and apply family-wise Bonferroni/Holm correction across the 4\\u20135 downstream tasks. Report effect sizes alongside p-values because raw PPL differences at low bits can be misleadingly small relative to PPL magnitudes.\\n\\n**Reconciliation with fixed requirements.** Weight-only? Yes \\u2014 primary DV focuses on weight quantization; activations stay FP16 at evaluation time (which is how QuaRot's headline ablations are reported). Bits \\u2208 {2, 3, 4}? Yes \\u2014 W4, W3, W2 are the cells; FP16 anchors and W8 is omitted because the question is explicitly about low-precision regimes. RTN + GPTQ baselines? Yes \\u2014 both are explicit cells, and QuaRot's Hadamard is the *treatment* layered on each. The design is therefore a clean 2 (method) \\u00d7 3 (bits) \\u00d7 2 (Hadamard on/off) full factorial on weight-only PTQ.\\n\\n### (3) Assumptions and Uncertainties\\n\\n**Assumptions** (proposed defaults; flag of which are negotiable):\\n- A1. \\\"Weight-only\\\" means weights are quantized at evaluation; **activations remain in FP16** for the primary analysis. (KV-cache quantization is **out of scope** by default \\u2014 confirm.)\\n- A2. Per-channel, symmetric, uniform quantization \\u2014 matches QuaRot's default RTN/GPTQ setup. Outlier clipping at \\u00b16\\u03c3 is QuaRot's published choice.\\n- A3. Calibration data: **C4**, English, with random subsampling.\\n- A4. Evaluation data: **WikiText-2** for PPL; **Hellaswag, ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, PIQA, WinoGrande** (optionally BoolQ) for downstream accuracy, via LM Eval Harness.\\n- A5. **Model = LLaMA-2-7B** as primary; LLaMA-2-13B as replication if allowed; optional TinyLlama-1.1B for pipeline validation.\\n- A6. \\\"Quality\\\" is operationalized as **both PPL and downstream accuracy**, with downstream accuracy as the primary endpoint at W2 where PPL becomes a coarser signal.\\n- A7. Random seeds for calibration sampling and (if applicable) the random Hadamard are fixed; results reported with bootstrap CIs.\\n- A8. All actual quantization and evaluation steps are **feasible only with external resources** (FER); this design describes what would be run, not what was run.\\n\\n**Uncertainties** (need researcher input or to be tracked in the design):\\n- U1. Whether the original QuaRot paper is the canonical baseline protocol reference (incl. its specific Hadamard insertion points \\u2014 pre- vs post-attention, KV-cache side, weight tying) or whether a different version of QuaRot applies. Recommend defaulting to the published paper and noting deviations.\\n- U2. Exact model family and sizes (e.g., LLaMA-2 vs LLaMA-3, 7B vs 13B vs 70B, or Mistral-7B). Smaller models are cheaper but may not exhibit the outlier pathology that motivates QuaRot.\\n- U3. Whether AWQ should be included as a tertiary baseline for context. (Not a fixed requirement; would clarify the literature positioning.)\\n- U4. Calibration data domain. Defaults to C4; an alternative (WikiText-2 calibration vs eval) or domain-specific calibration is possible but should be a deliberate choice.\\n- U5. **Outlier handling** under Hadamard-OFF cells. QuaRot specifically argues that outlier mitigation (clipping, percentile re-scaling) is *not* needed once Hadamard is applied; in the Hadamard-OFF cells we should follow the method's original protocol (RTN: clip at \\u00b13\\u03c3 per channel by convention; GPTQ: no clipping). Confirm.\\n- U6. **Statistical power at W2.** At very low bits, single-seed PPL can swing by tens of points depending on calibration order/seed for GPTQ; we mitigate via (a) \\u22653 calibration seeds, (b) paired bootstrap on \\u0394, (c) reporting effect sizes. Whether this is sufficient is itself an uncertainty \\u2014 recommend a sensitivity pre-registration.\\n- U7. **Hypothesis** (stated as expectation, not fact): *\\\"For weight-only quantization at identical (method, bits), adding QuaRot's Hadamard rotation reduces perplexity and improves downstream zero-shot accuracy; the improvement grows monotonically as bit-width decreases and is largest at W2 for the no-calibration method (RTN).\\\"* To test this, the central reported statistic should be **\\u0394 = (with-Hadamard) \\u2212 (without-Hadamard)** at fixed (method, bits), with sign convention made explicit (a more-negative \\u0394 indicates QuaRot beats the baseline on PPL).\\n- U8. Whether the activation-side Hadamard (H^T applied at runtime) is in scope as a true deployment wording, or simulation-only. Affects \\\"weight-only\\\" framing \\u2014 currently treated as FP16 activations + Hadamard rotation on W (a weight-only *quantization* but with the rotation panel applied \\u2014 this is exactly how the QuaRot paper argues the weight matrix change is the source of the gain).\\n\\n### Decisions I am asking you to confirm or redirect\\n1. Model family + sizes (default proposal: LLaMA-2-7B primary, LLaMA-2-13B replication, TinyLlama-1.1B smoke).\\n2. Whether KV-cache / activation quantization stay out of scope for this design.\\n3. Whether to include AWQ as a tertiary literature anchor (not required).\\n4. Calibration domain (default C4).\\n5. Hypothesized direction in U7 \\u2014 is the framing acceptable, or do you want the hypothesis to be two-sided (Hadamard helps on average but may hurt at some bits)?\\n6. Eval suite final list (default: WikiText-2 + {Hellaswag, ARC-e, ARC-c, PIQA, WinoGrande}).\\n\\nAfter your answer I will produce the **full design** in the `result` stage with a detailed protocol, statistical analysis plan, anticipated figures, explicit feasibility markers per step, and a controlled-ablation breakdown separating the rotation's contribution from the rest of the QuaRot pipeline.\", \"evidence\": \"Workspace inspection confirmed only `/workspace/task.json` is present; `task.json.documents.track.md` is the literal string `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` A global `find` returned no other relevant files. Runtime checks: Python 3.12.3, no `torch`/`transformers`/`datasets`/`numpy`/`scipy`, no GPU, 24 CPU cores, network curl returned `000 (no network)`. Therefore the QuaRot code, model weights, and calibration datasets are all **not available** locally; any training, quantization, or evaluation step is feasible only with external resources (FER).\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. Weight-only quantization scope: activations remain FP16 at eval; KV-cache quantization is out of scope by default.\", \"A2. Default RTN/GPTQ quantization is per-channel symmetric uniform with \\u00b16\\u03c3 clipping as in the QuaRot paper.\", \"A3. Calibration data default: C4 (en validation, random subsamples).\", \"A4. Evaluation default: WikiText-2 PPL + LM-Eval zero-shot suite {Hellaswag, ARC-e, ARC-c, PIQA, WinoGrande} (\\u00b1 BoolQ).\", \"A5. Model default: LLaMA-2-7B primary; LLaMA-2-13B replication; TinyLlama-1.1B smoke only.\", \"A6. Quality = both PPL and downstream accuracy; downstream accuracy is primary at W2 where PPL is coarser.\", \"A7. \\u22653 calibration seeds; Hadamard-seed fixed; paired bootstrap on \\u0394(Hadamard \\u2212 no-Hadamard); Holm correction across tasks.\", \"A8. All quantization + evaluation steps in the design are feasible only with external resources (FER); this deliverable is design-only.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1. Which QuaRot paper/protocol version is canonical (specific Hadamard insertion points and KV-cache handling).\", \"U2. Exact model family / size(s) to commit to for the primary cells.\", \"U3. Whether AWQ should be included as a tertiary literature baseline.\", \"U4. Calibration data domain choice beyond C4 default.\", \"U5. Outlier handling policy (no-clipping vs \\u00b13\\u03c3/\\u00b16\\u03c3) for the Hadamard-OFF cells.\", \"U6. Statistical power at W2 \\u2014 sensitivity analysis / pre-registration of seed count.\", \"U7. Whether the hypothesis is directional (QuaRot helps) or two-sided.\", \"U8. Whether the activation-side Hadamard (H^T at runtime) is in scope or treated as simulation-only within \\\"weight-only\\\" framing.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-15054299-bb8c-493f-b040-cbe9d316c095", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
